translate en b05_001:
    "Thursday afternoon. Drizzling again. Kai walked down the same dead-end alley, the shoulders of his jacket damp. He knew the routine by heart now: step in, hand over the cash, take the cassette, head home."

translate en b05_002:
    ageng "The usual?"

translate en b05_003:
    kai "The usual, Pak. {i}(hands over the money){/i} For tomorrow."

translate en b05_004:
    ageng "{i}(picks up a cassette labeled Friday and hands it over){/i} Here. Your Friday."

translate en b05_005:
    kai "Thanks, Pak. {i}(tucks the cassette into his bag with half a smile){/i} Once I've got this in my hands, walking home feels a little calmer."

translate en b05_006:
    kai_hati "A little. Never actually calm, not really. Still... it helps. Like carrying an umbrella. No matter how hard it pours tomorrow, at least I knew ahead of time."

translate en b05_007:
    "The rain had stopped completely. Outside, there were only crickets. Kai sat on the edge of his bed, Friday's cassette in hand. The ritual began: eject, pop the tape in, click."

translate en b05_008:
    kai_hati "Okay. Tomorrow's Friday. What's it gonna be? Probably Mom nagging me. Or Bu Sarah again. Or... whatever, let's just listen."

translate en b05_009a:
    "The cracked ear pads closed over both ears. The outside world vanished. Just him and the tape."

translate en b05_009b:
    "Kai left the headphones off. Let the cassette fill the room, he thought. At least he wouldn't be listening alone — even if his only company was a tinny little speaker."

translate en b05_010:
    "His finger pressed play."

translate en b05_011:
    kai_hati "Hiss first, same as usual. One... five... ten seconds. Wait, why isn't anyone talking yet?"

translate en b05_012:
    "At the fifteen-second mark, the hiss cut off. And what replaced it wasn't a conversation. Wasn't street noise. Wasn't anything at all."

translate en b05_013:
    kai "{i}(opens his eyes, frowning){/i} ...Huh?"

translate en b05_014:
    "He pressed the headphone cable in, twisted the jack inside the Walkman. Maybe it was loose. But the indicator light still glowed red. The tape reels were still spinning steadily behind the plastic window."

translate en b05_015:
    kai "The volume... {i}(rolls the volume wheel all the way up){/i} ...Nothing. Why is there nothing?!"

translate en b05_016:
    "No rush of wind. No rustling cloth. No distant car horn, no leaves, nothing at all. And the part that made his stomach go ice-cold: no breathing. On every cassette before this, that magic microphone had always picked up his breath. Always. Proof that he was still there."

translate en b05_017:
    "This time: a vacuum. Empty. Frozen."

translate en b05_018b:
    "The silence spilled across the entire bedroom — and somehow felt louder than any sound that had ever come out of that tiny speaker."

translate en b05_019:
    kai_hati "Don't stop spinning. Don't— I mean, keep going, something'll come on soon. Any second now—"

translate en b05_020:
    "Forty-five seconds of silence felt like forever. Kai ripped the headphones off with wildly shaking hands and threw them onto the bed, like the thing had just electrocuted him."

translate en b05_021:
    kai "{i}(gasping for air){/i} Hah... hah... no. No, no, no."

translate en b05_022:
    "The air in the room suddenly felt thin. Cold sweat ran down his temples. His mind, already always too loud, raced out of control, piecing together the only conclusion that made sense to a terrified teenager."

translate en b05_023:
    kai_hati "Those tapes always record tomorrow. Always. If tomorrow there's no voice of mine... no breath... {i}(swallows hard){/i} ...that means tomorrow I'm gone. Tomorrow I die."

translate en b06_001:
    "The bedroom suddenly felt airtight. The word \"die\" buzzed inside Kai's head, over and over, like a jammed tape."

translate en b06_002:
    kai_hati "Die. Tomorrow. How? A road accident? My heart gives out in class? Or does this ceiling collapse while I'm asleep? Which one? Which one?!"

translate en b06_003:
    "He needed an anchor. Something to tether his mind before it floated right off. With jerky, frantic movements, Kai snatched the Walkman off the bed. He violently ejected tomorrow's cassette and tossed it into the corner of the room, as if the plastic shell were radiating poison."

translate en b06_004:
    kai "{i}(rummaging through his desk drawer){/i} Blank tape... blank tape... where— there. Here."

translate en b06_005:
    "A worn-out spare tape he usually used to record random songs off the radio. He had to make sure the recorder still worked. He had to hear his own breath. Right now. As proof he was still alive."

translate en b06_006:
    "His hands were shaking too badly. His fingers were stiff and freezing. The cassette slipped out of the Walkman and clattered onto the floor. Kai dropped to his knees after it, breathing hard. His eyes stung — not from sadness, but from frustration and fear spilling past the brim."

translate en b06_007:
    kai "{i}(whispers, forcing his hands to stop){/i} Hold still... hold still, hands. Just this once. Stay still."

translate en b06_008:
    "Deep breath. This time the Walkman door snapped shut. His thumb pressed the red REC button and PLAY together. A loud click."

translate en b06_009:
    "Kai brought the Walkman close to his lips. He opened his mouth wide. He meant to shout his own name. Or sing any song at all. Anything, as long as his voice existed in this world."

translate en b06_010:
    "Nothing came out."

translate en b06_011:
    "His throat felt locked shut. His lips trembled, his jaw rigid. All that escaped were ragged breaths and choked sobs."

translate en b06_012:
    kai_hati "I can't. I can't talk. {i}(hugs the Walkman to his chest){/i} I'm scared. If I force a sound out, and then play it back... and all I hear is the same silence from before... I can't take it. I can't handle hearing that."

translate en b06_013:
    "The Walkman stopped recording. Kai stayed on the floor. His mind spun, grasping for one thing — just one — that could prove he really existed tonight."

translate en b06_014a:
    "His hand still trembling, Kai raised his phone toward the window. The Malang night sky was clear after the rain, stars peeking out one by one. Click. The photo saved — a little blurry from the shake, but real. The date and timestamp sat right in the corner of the screen."

translate en b06_015a:
    kai_hati "There. That's proof. Tonight, at this exact hour, I'm still here. I took this picture."

translate en b06_014b:
    "Kai reached for the small notebook on his desk and a pen nearly out of ink. He wrote in big, slanted, messy letters: {i}\"I'm still here. Thursday night. My hand is shaking, but this is my handwriting.\"{/i}"

translate en b06_015b:
    kai_hati "My handwriting. Nobody else wrote this. That means I'm... still here. Still here."

translate en b06_014c:
    "Kai didn't reach for anything. He just hugged his knees tighter. Fine. If there was no proof he could hold in his hands, at least this fear was real — and dead people can't be scared."

translate en b06_015c:
    kai_hati "...This fear is proof, right? I'm scared, so that means I'm still alive. Doesn't it?"

translate en b06_016:
    "That night stretched on forever. Kai curled up on his bedroom floor, clutching his knees tight. Knowing the schedule of your own death, it turned out, was the cruelest curse he had ever bought — and he'd bought it with his allowance."

translate en b07_001:
    "Morning had barely broken when Kai was already sprinting through the lingering mist. His breath came in gasps, his chest ready to burst — he didn't care. He shoved the shop door open so hard the bell jangled in panic."

translate en b07_002:
    kai "Pak! What is this supposed to mean?!"

translate en b07_003:
    "His voice cracked, echoing between the quiet shelves. He slammed the cassette labeled Friday onto the counter. His hands were still shaking. His face was puffy — he hadn't slept a wink all night."

translate en b07_004:
    kai "Why is this tape silent, Pak?! Why is there zero sound after the fifteen-second mark?! Did you sell me a broken tape?!"

translate en b07_005:
    "Pak Ageng didn't flinch. He didn't even stop wiping down an old tape deck behind the counter. His gaze was completely calm, as if Kai's anger were just a tiny ripple across a still pond."

translate en b07_006:
    ageng "{i}(pulls a banana-leaf packet from a bamboo basket under the counter and slides it over){/i} Eat first."

translate en b07_007:
    kai "Pak, I'm not—"

translate en b07_008:
    ageng "Eat first. An empty stomach only makes your head louder."

translate en b07_009:
    "Kai wanted to refuse. He wanted to keep yelling. Yet the warm, savory aroma of banana leaf and the old man's stillness disarmed his anger piece by piece. With shaking hands, he unwrapped the parcel: chicken lemper, still piping warm."

translate en b07_010:
    kai "{i}(takes a bite; chewy sticky rice and sweet-savory chicken fill his mouth){/i} ..."

translate en b07_011:
    "For a brief second, last night's terror eased. There was a real taste on his tongue. His feet were still planted on the ground."

translate en b07_012:
    "Once he swallowed the last bite, Pak Ageng picked up the cassette and turned it over between his wrinkled fingers."

translate en b07_013:
    ageng "Tapes never lie, kid. {i}(looks straight into Kai's eyes){/i} But human ears mishear things all the time. You heard that silence as an ending. Even though silence can mean plenty of things."

translate en b07_014:
    kai "What do you mean, plenty of things, Pak? It's empty. Completely empty. No breathing, no—"

translate en b07_015:
    ageng "Silence doesn't always mean there's nothing there. Sometimes it's a pause. Sometimes... it's the peak."

translate en b07_016:
    "Kai stared at the cassette in Pak Ageng's hand. His head still fought against it. Yet the weight on his chest — just a fraction — shifted. Now it was Kai's turn to decide: what was that silence?"

translate en b07_017a:
    "Pak Ageng didn't argue. Didn't agree either. He simply got up, walked over to the radio in the corner of the shop, and switched it off."

translate en b07_018a:
    ageng "{i}(softly){/i} Quiet in here too, isn't it? Yet we're still right here."

translate en b07_019a:
    "Kai didn't answer. The silence of the shop and the silence of the tape stacked right on top of each other in his ears."

translate en b07_017b:
    ageng "{i}(smiles faintly){/i} See? Try listening again. Silence isn't the end of a song, kid. Even a good song has a pause — that's the exact part where people hold their breath."

translate en b07_018b:
    "Pak Ageng slipped the cassette into the shop's player and pressed play. Hiss... then silence. Only this time, Kai didn't cover his ears. For the first time, he listened to the silence all the way through — and wasn't afraid."

translate en b07_019b:
    kai "{i}(lets out a long breath){/i} ...It's over. Turns out I can listen to the whole thing."

translate en b07_017c:
    kai "I don't want to think about it anymore. Seriously. I'm exhausted."

translate en b07_018c:
    "Kai took the cassette off the counter... then, without a word, walked to the very back shelf of the shop and tucked it away among the dustiest tapes. As far out of sight as possible."

translate en b07_019c:
    ageng "{i}(watches, not stopping him){/i} Wes. If that's what you need right now."

translate en b07_020c:
    "Still, Kai knew — and Pak Ageng knew too — that he'd be avoiding that shelf for weeks."

translate en b07_021c:
    ageng "Go on home. Live your Friday. Don't let your fear make you deaf before the sound even gets here."

translate en b07_022c:
    kai "{i}(gives a half-bow){/i} ...Yeah, Pak. Thanks for the lemper."

translate en b07_021:
    ageng "Go on home. Live your Friday. {i}(hands the cassette back){/i} Don't let your fear make you deaf before the sound even gets here."

translate en b07_022:
    kai "{i}(slips the cassette into his pocket, giving a half-bow){/i} ...Yeah, Pak. Thanks for the lemper."

translate en b07_023:
    "Behind Kai, Pak Ageng's rocking chair started creaking again. Slow. Steady. Like time in that shop really did move at its own pace — and for this morning, that pace was just enough to help Kai walk home."

translate en b08_001:
    "Before actually heading out, Kai stopped near a low shelf beside the counter. Sitting there, tied with frayed raffia string, was a bundle of old letters. Yellowed paper, handwritten envelopes. The very top envelope was slightly open."

translate en b08_002a:
    "Kai glanced at Pak Ageng — the old man was pretending to stay busy in his rocking chair. Slowly, Kai slid out the top envelope. Inside, the letter was written in neat, upright handwriting by a much younger hand."

translate en b08_002a_p2:
    "Its contents were simple: news about a radio that had just been fixed, about the rain in this city refusing to stop, and a single underlined sentence — {i}\"Wait for me to come home. We'll listen to it together.\"{/i}"

translate en b08_002a_p3:
    "No sender's name, just a single initial at the bottom. Kai carefully folded it back exactly the way he found it. All at once, Pak Ageng didn't just feel like some strange shopkeeper — he had been young once, had waited once, had been waited for."

translate en b08_003a:
    kai_hati "...Who wrote this? Or... who was it for? {i}(shakes his head){/i} None of my business. Weirdly, though, this shop feels a little more... human now."

translate en b08_002b:
    kai_hati "Those are someone else's letters. Not meant for me. {i}(pulls his hand back){/i} I wouldn't want anyone reading my letters either."

translate en b08_003b:
    "Kai straightened the crooked raffia tie — nothing more — and turned toward the door. Behind him, the rocking chair paused its creaking for a beat. Pak Ageng didn't say a word. Still, he seemed to have noticed."

translate en b08_004:
    "Ever since getting back from the shop, time felt like a countdown ticking inside his skull. Dust dancing under the ceiling light, hairline cracks in the plaster, the ticking clock — everything felt like a silent witness. Kai no longer looked at his bedroom as a safe place. It was a tiny museum, and he was about to leave it behind."

translate en b08_005:
    kai_hati "If I'm really gone tomorrow... I don't want Mom finding my room a total mess like this. Don't want people seeing... all these scattered pieces of me."

translate en b08_006:
    "At midnight, he started. Big cardboard boxes dragged out from under the bed. One by one, he sorted through seventeen years of his life."

translate en b08_007:
    kai "{i}(lifts a stack of yellowed gaming magazines){/i} These... trash. {i}(picks up a jar full of candy wrappers){/i} This too. Used to think it was so precious. Turns out it's just junk I stored neatly."

translate en b08_008:
    "Scrap paper covered in unfinished angsty song lyrics — into the black trash bag. A middle-school diary packed with childish complaints — in. Old goofy-faced photos — in. And at the bottom of the drawer: a stack of love letters he'd never had the guts to send to anyone, all these years."

translate en b08_009:
    kai "{i}(holds the stack of letters for a long moment){/i} ...These too. Never gonna get sent anyway, right? {i}(drops them gently into the black bag){/i} At least... I want to be remembered as a tidier version of myself."

translate en b08_010:
    "His room looked bigger now. Emptier. Kai sat at his study desk, pulled out a few clean sheets of white paper and a black pen with plenty of ink left. He started writing. Not homework. Not diary entries. Goodbye letters — ones he'd never actually send."

translate en b08_011:
    kai_hati "For Mom first. {i}(writing){/i} Sorry, Bu... I know I shut myself off too much. Hard to read. Always wrapped up in old junk you didn't think mattered..."

translate en b08_012:
    "His hand shook a little on the last sentence, but he finished it:"

translate en b08_013:
    kai "{i}(softly reading his own handwriting aloud){/i} \"Thank you for letting me live in my own little world, even though you never really understood what was inside it.\""

translate en b08_014:
    "The second letter was for himself — his younger self. An apology for burning through so much time panicking about the future that he forgot how to breathe easy in the present."

translate en b08_015:
    kai_hati "Sorry, old Kai. We spent way too much time being scared. {i}(smiles bitterly){/i} Even though ordinary days were... actually pretty okay."

translate en b08_016:
    "He folded the letters neatly and tucked them into the bottom desk drawer, behind a stack of blank cassettes. Maybe nobody would ever read them. Maybe they'd rot in there. Still, to Kai, writing was one last way to leave a trace of his voice on paper — before the silence he'd heard finally came to collect him."

translate en b08_017:
    kai_hati "{i}(rests his head down on the desk){/i} ...Done. My room's clean. My letters are written. If tomorrow really is my last day... {i}(closes his eyes){/i} ...at least I said goodbye. Quietly."

translate en b08_018:
    "That night, for the first time in ages, Kai fell asleep in a spotless room. Yet his chest stayed tight with an echo that refused to fade."
